WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
It always kind of throws me for a loop&nbsp;
that this movie starts in Chinatown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.400
because it’s the one part of the film that&nbsp;
doesn’t look… I don’t know… Christmasy?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.160
The portrayal of Asain-American relations in&nbsp;
Gremlins is actually the key to understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.520
the main message of this film, but I’ll&nbsp;
get into that later on in the video. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.800
I want to translate these Chinese characters&nbsp;
and see if there’s anything interesting written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.720
here. For the majority of the opening, there is&nbsp;
not, but the store that Mr. Peltzer goes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.280
where he eventually buys the Mogwai  is called “Magic”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.360
and as he goes down the stairs, the characters&nbsp;
on the walls mean “God” and “Strange”.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.200
The word Mogwai also means evil spirit, so&nbsp;
if you’re fluent in Cantonese, you already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.640
knew that this shop is no ordinary shop, and Billy&nbsp;
Peltzer’s new pet is no ordinary little creature.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.560
- [Mr. Peltzer] Look, I've gotta have him. It’s a present for my son for Christmas.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.680
- What are you talking about. Your son already has&nbsp;
an adorable dog? But like this store in Chinatown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.000
the movie itself is filled with many mysterious&nbsp;
secrets. If you want to learn why all of Mr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.520
Peltzer’s wacky inventions are meaningful,&nbsp;
who this guy in the giant portrait is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.134
or the true motivation of this drunk guy’s rants,&nbsp;
then stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:56.444 --> 00:01:00.886
(metal music)

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.840
It’s that time of year again. December&nbsp;
25th. This Jew is going to the movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.120
But first I’m gonna talk about a movie.&nbsp;
And that movie is. Called. Final Fantasy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.240
Advent Children. Just kidding. Though&nbsp;
I do like that movie though. It’s pretty cool.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.320
My name is CZsWorld and today, we’re doing&nbsp;
Gremlins. Gremlins was directed by Joe Dante&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.000
and released in summer 1984, you already know what&nbsp;
Gremlins is. Let’s get into the Things You Missed.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.840
(knives slashing)
(Christmas music)

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.840
We are first introduced to the setting of&nbsp;
Gremlins through this billboard that says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.240
Rockin’ Ricky Rialto Whips Out the Oldies, and&nbsp;
it’s obviously a parody of Indiana Jones. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.840
is one of many examples of references to&nbsp;
Stephen Spielberg, who directed Indiana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.120
Jones and was the executive producer on&nbsp;
Gremlins. As we’re going through the town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.000
the movie theater marquee has two titles, A Boy’s&nbsp;
Life and Watch the Skies. A Boy’s Life was at one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.320
time a working title for Stephen Spielberg’s&nbsp;
E.T. The Extra-Terrestrial and Watch the Skies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.240
was at one point a working title for Stephen&nbsp;
Spielberg’s Close Encounters of the Third Kind.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.320
We also see one of the Gremlins utter the line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.360
“phone home ca-ca” before cutting&nbsp;
the phone lines in Billy’s house...

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.320
- Phone home ca-ca!

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.970
- Which is a reference to the&nbsp;
most famous line from ET.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:10.640
- ET phone home!

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.000
- We also see an ET toy in the department&nbsp;
store where the finale takes place.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.920
In Billy’s room there’s a&nbsp;
poster for Twilight Zone:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.880
The Movie, which was a sci-fi horror&nbsp;
anthology film based on The Twilight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.400
Zone TV show that featured segments from&nbsp;
both Joe Dante and Stephen Spielberg.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.920
Billy’s father is an inventor.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.080
- Now let’s say you’re late for the&nbsp;
big meeting. It’s really important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.840
You’re on your way in, you reach&nbsp;
up. *gasp* You forgot to shave.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.800
- Uhh… I’ll ignore that.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.560
But later in the movie, on Christmas eve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.320
Mr. Peltzer uses the phone booth at an&nbsp;
invention convention, and Steven Spielberg can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.360
be seen crossing frame with a broken&nbsp;
foot in some kind of powered wheelchair.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.540
Those are all the Stephen King...

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.040
*BEEP*

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.320
Those are all the Spielberg easter eggs&nbsp;
I noticed. There are probably others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.520
so let me know in the comments.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.640
It is Christmas season in the town&nbsp;
of Kingston Falls. As we see when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.560
this kid smashes a snowball on this girl’s head.

00:02:56.184 --> 00:02:57.847
 Holy sh*t that was brutal. 

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.400
Kingston Falls is definitely an homage to&nbsp;
Bedford Falls from the classic Christmas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.560
flick It’s a Wonderful Life. We actually see Mrs.&nbsp;
Peltzer watching that movie when Billy comes home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.600
and the part we see is George Bailey&nbsp;
running by the movie theater marquee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.680
much like what we just saw&nbsp;
Billy doing in Gremlins.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.760
There are a ton of other similarities. Both main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.720
characters work at a bank. Both have&nbsp;
human antagonists who are money-hungry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.080
holiday grouches who control the town&nbsp;
through their real estate practices.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.400
- [Kate] We’re trying to have Dorry’s&nbsp;
Pub declared a landmark. Mrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.920
Deagle’s trying to take his lease away.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.640
- At one point we see one of the&nbsp;
financially insolvent citizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.920
bargaining with the guy that runs the bank,&nbsp;
another parallel to It’s A Wonderful Life.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.040
And just the look and feel of these two&nbsp;
towns are very similar in a way that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.840
can’t really put into words.&nbsp;
Also, in Mrs. Deagle’s house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.332
there's this huge portrait by the stairs of Edward Arnold,

00:03:38.332 --> 00:03:41.653
who was known for working with Frank Capra, the director of It's A Wonderful Life.

00:03:41.707 --> 00:03:44.644
Edward was at one point considered for playing the role of Potter.

00:03:44.644 --> 00:03:48.800
What makes Gremlins so cool though, is that it’s&nbsp;
not just a Christmas movie, it’s not just a horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.560
movie, but it’s also got elements of science&nbsp;
fiction, some of which you may have noticed.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.840
Everyone knows there are three rules when&nbsp;
it comes to the Mogwai: no bright lights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.600
don’t get them wet and don’t feed them after&nbsp;
midnight… it’s never really explained what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.680
time it’s OK to feed them again, or if they&nbsp;
follow Daylight Savings time, but whatever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.200
When you feed them too late they begin their&nbsp;
metamorphosis into Gremlins. It starts when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.800
they go into their cocoons, which are awfully&nbsp;
reminiscent of the eggs in the Alien movies.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.080
In the spot where the cocoons form, there’s&nbsp;
an issue of the famous horror movie magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.360
Fangoria, on the ground, so we know that&nbsp;
this is where the horror is about to begin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.480
The movie is literally filled&nbsp;
with sci-fi horror references.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.160
The first example is way back at the beginning&nbsp;
of the movie, while Billy is rushing to work.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.245
- [Billy] Hey Dr. Moreau.

00:04:26.245 --> 00:04:27.012
- [Dr. Moreau] Morning Billy!

00:04:27.012 --> 00:04:29.914
- We later see Dr. Moreau’s&nbsp;
office, he is a veterinarian,

00:04:29.914 --> 00:04:33.520
which is really funny because this is&nbsp;
a reference to HG Wells’s 1896 novel:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.160
The Island of Dr. Moreau, which is a story about&nbsp;
a mad scientist who creates human-animal hybrids.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.920
There is a moment when Billy’s Mom is fighting&nbsp;
off Gremlins and she realizes something is moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:45.840
in one of the Christmas stockings. She cuts&nbsp;
it open to reveal it was only this robot toy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.440
I don’t really know what this is doing here.&nbsp;
I mean their only son is at least of out high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.880
school now. Like he has a job at the bank,&nbsp;
I don’t really know who this toy is for.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.840
Remember that convention scene where we&nbsp;
spotted Stephen Spielberg? Well there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.200
a couple more details to talk about in&nbsp;
that shot. Actually, quite a few more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.045
including another cameo that may surprise you.

00:05:07.316 --> 00:05:10.880
(ambient music)

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.560
Please direct your attention to the man&nbsp;
on the right. This is Jerry Goldsmith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.880
the composer of the score of Gremlins, but what I really&nbsp;
want to point out is this guy, Robby the Robot.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.200
Robby initially comes from the 1956 sci-fi&nbsp;
classic Forbidden Planet. In the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.680
there’s another HG Wells reference. We can&nbsp;
see the time machine from his 1895 novel:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.680
The Time Machine. The look of the machine&nbsp;
is based on the 1960 movie adaptation. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.640
be totally honest, I only know what this&nbsp;
looks like because of that one episode of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.720
Futurama with the Forwards Time Machine,&nbsp;
whose design is based off the 1960 movie.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.360
When we cut away to a different scene,&nbsp;
and then come back to the convention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.560
the time machine is now gone, with&nbsp;
only a puff of smoke left in its wake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.120
so I guess it’s operational.&nbsp;
That’s a fun background detail.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.960
Other sci-fi references include a poster for&nbsp;
the dystopian movie The Road Warrior next to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.480
Billy’s TV, maybe a clue that Gizmo would&nbsp;
be getting behind the wheel before long.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.720
Then there are a bunch of nods to 1950s sci-fi&nbsp;
comics and movies, and I think there may be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:02.960
reason that they focus on this era so much. We&nbsp;
see comics like Horrific and House of Terror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.000
the latter of which requires 3D glasses&nbsp;
which were popular during that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.000
period. Billy watches the movie Invasion&nbsp;
of the Body Snatchers from 1956, another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.840
film that features alien pods similar to the&nbsp;
cocoons in which the Mogwai become Gremlins.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:17.440
After Pete calls into Rockin Ricky’s radio&nbsp;
show to report the outbreak of Gremlins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.640
Ricky broadcasts his frustrations with&nbsp;
all the calls from Kingston Falls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.160
- Rockin Ricky’s getting fed up with all&nbsp;
this Orson Welles crap, so cool it will ya?

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.840
- He’s referring to the famous radio production&nbsp;
of War of the Worlds that was put on by Orson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.000
Welles in 1938. This production was so believable&nbsp;
that people that radio listeners actually thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.560
there was an alien invasion going on. War of&nbsp;
the Worlds was turned into a movie in 1953.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.720
So what is the deal with all of these&nbsp;
1950s and 60s science fiction references?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.120
There are a lot of them. This, I think, plays&nbsp;
into the overall meaning of the movie Gremlins.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.200
The period of time following the 1945 test&nbsp;
detonation of the first nuclear weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.520
is referred to as The Atomic Age. This blast did&nbsp;
not only have a lasting effect on the area where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.680
it was detonated in New Mexico, but there&nbsp;
was also a *cultural* residual radiation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.320
and a new wave of science fiction&nbsp;
properties flooded the zeitgeist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.920
most notably Godzilla, but also the other&nbsp;
titles I’ve pointed out in this video.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:07.680
If you need more proof that Joe Dante was&nbsp;
specifically referencing the Atomic Era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.280
I noticed a lot. Billy has a poster&nbsp;
for Agars Atomic Pest Control in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.000
room. I don’t know exactly what that&nbsp;
is, but it seems like in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.080
the Gremlins are considered the metaphorical&nbsp;
“Atomic Pests”. We even see Mrs. Peltzer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.440
using flea and tick killer spray to&nbsp;
exterminate some of the Gremlins later on.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.080
The nuclear bomb that United States set off to&nbsp;
begin the Atomic Age was named The Gadget. A slang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.160
term for a gadget, is a gizmo -- which happens&nbsp;
to be the name of our poster boy for this movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.400
Also, Mr. Peltzer is an inventor who makes all&nbsp;
kinds of wacky gadgets -- we’ll get into that.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.680
The second rule is to never get the&nbsp;
Mogwai wet. When exposed to water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.520
they multiply, and the same rule applies&nbsp;
after they’ve metamorphosed into Gremlins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.280
We see the leader of the Gremlins, Stripe,&nbsp;
jump into a swimming pool at the YMCA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.960
and this causes some kind of reaction,&nbsp;
causing the water to bubble and turn green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.560
giving it the appearance of radioactive&nbsp;
material, also known as atomic waste.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.240
The Gremlins take over Dorry’s Pub and&nbsp;
get a little wild and make a huge mess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.440
of the place. When the Dorry’s logo gets&nbsp;
knocked over it looks like a radiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.640
hazard symbol. I know people are going to&nbsp;
say I’m just looking too much into this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.120
but we do see this a couple&nbsp;
times in different places.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.560
Then there’s the first rule of the Mogwai&nbsp;
-- they hate bright lights. On more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.000
one occasion we see the Gremlins’ biggest&nbsp;
weakness is a bright flash -- maybe like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.640
the bright flash that occurs&nbsp;
when an atomic bomb goes off.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.320
(EXPLOSION MEME)

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.520
There’s even a part where Mrs. Peltzer&nbsp;
puts one of the Gremlins in the microwave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:24.080
and starts it. A microwave transfers heat using&nbsp;
electromagnetic radiation. A much larger scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.840
example of something that uses radiation&nbsp;
to transfer energy is an atomic bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.720
the thing that The United States&nbsp;
dropped on Japan during World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.891
II. Like Hiroshima and Nagasaki, this&nbsp;
gremlin explodes.

00:08:34.892 --> 00:08:36.547
(grunts and flying guts)

00:08:37.584 --> 00:08:38.375
*ding*

00:08:38.592 --> 00:08:39.493
Food’s ready!

00:08:39.493 --> 00:08:43.600
If you’re familiar at all with 20th Century&nbsp;
History, you probably know why these nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:46.480
weapons were developed, it was because&nbsp;
of growing international tensions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:50.640
World War II. And that brings me back to the&nbsp;
original lore of what a Gremlin actually is.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.800
A Gremlin is an imaginary mischievous sprite&nbsp;
regarded as responsible for an unexplained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.200
problem or fault, especially a mechanical or&nbsp;
electronic one. The term originated as slang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.360
in the Royal Air Force to denote a mischievous&nbsp;
creature that sabotaged World War II aircrafts.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.840
Dante even kind of confirms that the Gremlins in&nbsp;
his movie are in fact an allegory for World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:11.200
II Gremlins by including a moment in the pub&nbsp;
scene where one of the Gremlins shoots a gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.360
at Kate and misses, instead hitting this photo&nbsp;
of what appears to be a World War II aircraft.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.720
Throughout the movie, it is not just war&nbsp;
machines that get taken over by Gremlins. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.640
all machinery. The first example is Billy’s car,&nbsp;
which won’t start when he needs to get to work.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:25.280
- [Mr. Futterman] These goddamn foreign cars,&nbsp;
they always freeze up on you.”

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:29.120
- [Zac] Billy’s next door neighbor Mr. Futterman spends&nbsp;
the entire movie on a tirade against foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.440
machinery. One evening, he got drunk because he&nbsp;
found foreign machine parts in his snow-plow.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.960
- [Mr. Futterman] How’d you like to bring your plow in for a&nbsp;
tune-up and find it loaded with foreign parts?

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.120
- The movie seems to have a theme about fear&nbsp;
of overreliance on foreign made products.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.600
Particularly, products made in Asain countries&nbsp;
like China and Japan. The 1980s, when the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.440
takes place, were the years that China really&nbsp;
emerged as a manufacturing powerhouse and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.280
and more technology and machinery started to&nbsp;
be made there, according to St. Louis Fed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.840
This led to a fear in the United States that China&nbsp;
would surpass the West’s technological dominance.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.360
- [Mr. Futterman] You gotta watch out for foreigners because&nbsp;
they plant Gremlins in our machinery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:09.360
The same Gremlins that brought down our planes&nbsp;
in the big one. That’s right. World War II.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.280
- Interestingly, this isn’t the only time we&nbsp;
hear a character use the phrase: The Big One.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.480
- [Rand] I bet every kid in America would like&nbsp;
one of these. They might even replace the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:22.880
dog as the family pet. The&nbsp;
Peltzer Pet. This could really be the big one.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:26.880
- In this instance, the pets serve as a metaphor&nbsp;
for the rise of Chinese tech. The Peltzer family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:31.200
already has a perfectly good dog, Barney. I’m&nbsp;
not the best at identifying dog breeds, but if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.200
I had to guess there’s some terrier in there and&nbsp;
maybe some golden retriever. And these are both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.360
English breeds. We see Barney get jealous that he&nbsp;
now has competition from this Chinese creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:43.120
and when the Mogwai multiply, they terrorize&nbsp;
him and string him up in Christmas lights.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:47.200
When Gizmo first joins the family, everyone&nbsp;
loves him, but as he multiplies and takes over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:52.320
it becomes a big problem. Mr. Peltzer, an American&nbsp;
inventor, has not mastered his own products yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.840
but he’s already trying to figure out how&nbsp;
to make money by selling Chinese Gizmos.

00:10:56.576 --> 00:10:58.480
- You do with Mogwai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:05.440
what your society has done with all of nature’s&nbsp;
gifts. You are not ready.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.200
- The only one who seems to understand the danger&nbsp;
of an economy relying on foreign products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.400
is Mr. Futterman, the same guy who&nbsp;
has a Richard Nixon campaign poster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:17.118
hanging in his house a decade after Nixon’s&nbsp;
resignation as President of the United States.

00:11:22.106 --> 00:11:26.114
(patriotic music)

00:11:26.114 --> 00:11:32.400
- We seek friendly relations with all nations.&nbsp;
Any nation can be our friend. Without being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:37.680
any other nation’s enemy. I have taken this&nbsp;
action, because of my profound conviction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:41.360
that all nations will gain&nbsp;
from a reduction of tensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:45.840
and a better relationship between the United&nbsp;
States and the People’s Republic of China.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:49.280
Richard Nixon’s relations with China are&nbsp;
well recognized as one of the most meaningful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.280
parts of his presidency. Now I’m no more an&nbsp;
expert on politics than I am on dog-breeds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:56.560
but from what I understand, Nixon was&nbsp;
instrumental in opening up more trade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.240
between the US and China. In his book,&nbsp;
Seize the Moment, he wrote: “If we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.000
to have an impact on the changes occurring in&nbsp;
China, we should not pull the plug on trade.”

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:07.760
His goal was for American companies to have a&nbsp;
presence in China, but what actually happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:11.120
was American companies began producing&nbsp;
goods in Chinese factories to save money.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:13.200
But perhaps Mr. Futterman initially supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:16.000
Nixon because of his intention to take&nbsp;
advantage of free trade with China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.840
but now he's grown tired of seeing made&nbsp;
in China all over everything he buys.

00:12:21.449 --> 00:12:25.280
- You know they’re still shipping them&nbsp;
over here. They put them in the cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.600
they put them in the TVs, they put them in the&nbsp;
stereos and the radios you stick in your ears.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:32.560
They put them in your watches, they got&nbsp;
little teeny Gremlins for your watches.

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:36.160
- As Billy walks Kate home that evening, she&nbsp;
even hints that the reason for his rage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:40.080
against foreign products may be related&nbsp;
to the relocation of jobs overseas.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:45.440
- He’s been like that ever since he lost his&nbsp;
job. He’s like a lot of people around here.

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:49.440
- It seems a lot of citizens are blissfully unaware&nbsp;
of the problem, like the Deputy at the police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:53.200
station. When Billy comes to warn him that the&nbsp;
Gremlins have taken over, he's playing with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:57.200
little cowboy game called The Western Man and he&nbsp;
doesn't believe the story about The Gremlins being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:01.200
a threat. This could represent the typical&nbsp;
American stereotype, that many Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:04.720
believe they are the center of the world and&nbsp;
aren't concerned with international headlines.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:09.200
He sees Gizmo get tangled up in an American flag,&nbsp;
and totally doesn't read the situation correctly.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.520
- [Sheriff Frank] Patriotic little fella ain’t he?&nbsp;
Waving a flag and everything.

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:17.680
- I don't know about you, but to me, Gizmo does&nbsp;
NOT look happy holding the American flag.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:19.760
When given the chance Mogwai gravitate towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.960
Asian products. Like a Japanese&nbsp;
video game, or motor company.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:27.200
The Gremlins also mess with electronics throughout&nbsp;
their takeover of the town by cutting phone lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:30.640
manipulating stop lights, messing&nbsp;
with antennae, jacking a snow plow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:32.636
screwing with the controls of a chair lift…

00:13:32.636 --> 00:13:33.833
(woman screaming)

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:36.480
…cutting a police cruiser’s&nbsp;
brake line, blowing fuses all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.000
over the place and broadcasting to&nbsp;
themselves like it’s YouTube 2006.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.280
Finally, the fate of the Gremlins also&nbsp;
plays into this metaphor. Before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:45.840
Mogwai turn into Gremlins and&nbsp;
become the main antagonists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:49.120
we’re faced with another antagonist,&nbsp;
the grouchy miser, Mrs. Deagle.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:52.960
She has something against Billy’s dog and&nbsp;
publicly expresses her desire to put him down.

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:56.720
- I’ll put him in my spin-dryer on high heat!”

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:01.280
- So yeah, she can die. Billy can be seen after that&nbsp;
blowing off some steam by drawing a caricature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:06.000
of her as a dragon being slain by a sword. This&nbsp;
establishes the idea that dragons represent evil.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:10.640
But that’s not the first dragon we see. There’s&nbsp;
also one in the shop where Rand first purchases…

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:12.320
- An interesting choice of words.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.160
- …Where he first obtains the Mogwai.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:19.920
- [Rand]] You got yourself a bad case of&nbsp;
dragonbreath. (gong) Bad breath.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:23.760
- So that’s a couple examples of dragons being&nbsp;
portrayed negatively. Well, when the Mogwai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.360
turn into Gremlins, which happens if they eat&nbsp;
after midnight, perhaps giving themselves that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:31.040
“dragonbreath”, they develop scales, and&nbsp;
look very reptilian, much like a dragon.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:34.240
For those reasons, it’s only appropriate&nbsp;
that Billy fights one of them off using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:37.680
the sword hanging on the wall by his&nbsp;
front door. It’s just like his drawing.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:41.040
However, the majority of the Gremlins end up&nbsp;
in the movie theater. With all of the ranting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.720
from Mr. Futterman about Gremlins getting into&nbsp;
the machinery and causing bi-planes to crash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:49.520
during World War II, it is only fitting that the&nbsp;
Americans get back at them with a big explosion.

00:14:49.879 --> 00:14:51.879
(explosion)

